      "message": "This trusts `record.key` as the library\u0027s own unit, but nothing ties `record.key` to the library\u0027s identity, so a crafted artifact can choose what \"own\" means and call another library\u0027s tables.\n\nProbed in a scratch copy of this commit: patched `(test evil)`\u0027s `library.key` from `lib:(test evil)` to `lib:(test greet)` (re-encoded with `Writer::str` over the walker\u0027s `library.key` field) and ran `(import (scheme base) (test evil)) dummy` — it loads with `ran: 1`, so `key !\u003d identity` is accepted. Then set evil\u0027s `library.key` to `lib:(test stx)` and replaced evil\u0027s `invoke` section with `(test stx)`\u0027s (which calls `%syntax-build` with `lib:(test stx)`): a program importing both loads with `ran`, even though evil\u0027s code names another library\u0027s unit and the message claims such a call \"is refused\". The check passes because the unit argument equals the spoofed `record.key`.\n\nFix: on load, before this check, refuse unless `record.key \u003d\u003d identity` (libraries compile with `key: identity.to_owned()` when `next_hashed` is false), and add a hostile test that patches `library.key` to another library\u0027s key.",
